\id JDG - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.2.20
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Judges
\toc1 The accounts of Various Heroes and Guides
\toc2 Judges
\mt2 The accounts of
\mt1 Various Heroes and Guides
\mt3 commonly called
\mt2 Judges
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 Judah and Simeon capture Adoni-Vezek
\p
\v 1 After Joshua’s death, the Israelites asked Yahweh, “Which tribe should go first to attack the Canaanites?”
\v 2 “Judah will go first,” Yahweh answered. “Listen, I’ve already determined that they’ll conquer the land.”
\p
\v 3 Then the Judah \add leaders\add* said to the Simeon \add tribe\add*, “Come with us into the region that we’ve been allocated and we can fight together against the Canaanites. Then we’ll do the same in your area.” So the warriors from the two tribes worked together.
\v 4 When they attacked, Yahweh gave them victory over the Canaanites and the Perizim and they killed ten thousand men at Bezek.
\v 5 They confronted Adoni-Vezek in Bezek and fought against him (then \add eventually\add* defeating the Canaanites and the Perizim).
\v 6 Adoni-Vezek \add tried to\add* flee but they chased him and captured him, cutting off his thumbs and big toes.
\v 7 “Seventy kings had their thumbs and big toes chopped off,” Adoni-Vezek said, “and were gathering scraps of food beneath my table. Just as I have done, so God has repaid to me.” Then they took him to Jerusalem and he died there.
\s1 The capture of Jerusalem and Hebron
\p
\v 8 The warriors from Judah attacked Jerusalem and captured the city, killing the inhabitants and setting the city on fire.
\v 9 Then they fought against the Canaanites who lived in the hill country, in the southern \add wilderness\add*, and in the \add western\add* foothills.
\v 10 They also fought the Canaanites in Kiryat-Arba (now known as Hebron) and killed Sheshay, Ahiman, and Talmay.
\s1 Otniel captures Devir
\r (Josh. 15:13-19)
\p
\v 11 From there they fought against Kiryat-Sefer (now known as Devir).
\v 12 Caleb \add had\add* said, “The one who attacks Kiryat-Sefer and captures it can marry my daughter Aksah.”
\v 13 Otniel (son of Caleb’s younger brother Kenaz) captured it so Caleb gave his daughter Aksah to him to become his wife.
\v 14 After the wedding, she urged Otniel to ask her father for a field. When she got off her donkey, Caleb asked her, “What can I do for you?”
\v 15 “Give me a blessing,” she requested. “Although you’ve given me land in the southern wilderness, give me some springs of water as well.” So Caleb gave her the upper springs and the lower springs.
\s1 The victory of descendants of Judah and Benjamin
\p
\v 16 The Keynim (descendants of Moses’ father-in-law), left the city of palm trees \add (Jericho\add* \add )\add* and went with Judah’s descendants to the southern wilderness near Arad and lived with the people \add there\add*.
\v 17 Then the warriors of Judah and Simeon conquered the Canaanites living at Tsefat and destroyed the city. \add Now\add* it’s called Hormah \add (which means ‘complete destruction’)\add*.
\v 18 They also captured Gaza, Ashkelon, and Ekron and \add all\add* their surrounding territories.
\v 19 Yahweh helped Judah and they took possession of the hill country, although they weren’t able to conquer the people living down in the plains because they had iron on their chariots.
\v 20 They gave Hebron to Caleb just as Moses had directed, and he dispossessed the three sons of the Anak from there.
\v 21 However Benjamin’s descendants didn’t drive the Jebusites living in Jerusalem out, so they have lived among the Benyamites in Jerusalem to this day.
\s1 Ephraim and Manasseh conquer Bethel
\p
\v 22 Also Joseph’s descendants attacked Bethel, and Yahweh helped them.
\v 23 They sent spies out to Bethel (previously called Luz)
\v 24 and they saw a man coming out of the city and told him, “Please show us an entrance into the city, and we’ll be kind to you.”
\v 25 So he showed them an entrance, and their warriors \add attacked and\add* killed the inhabitants, but they let the man and his family go free.
\v 26 The man went to the land of the Hittites and founded a city and called it Luz (which it’s still called to this day).
\s1 The locals not driven out by the Israelites
\p
\v 27 However Manasseh’s \add descendants\add* didn’t drive out those living in Beyt-Shean, Ta’anak, Dor, Yivleam, or Megiddo, because the Canaanites were determined to keep living there.
\v 28 (\add Later,\add* the Israelites became stronger and they forced the Canaanites to work for them as their slaves, but they didn’t drive them out.)
\v 29 Nor did Ephraim’s \add descendants\add* drive the Canaanites out of Gezer, so they continued to live among them.
\p
\v 30 Zebulun’s \add descendants\add* didn’t drive the Canaanites out of Kitron or Nahalal, so they continued to live among them and were forced to work as slaves.
\p
\v 31 Asher’s \add descendants\add* didn’t drive out those who were living in Ako, Sidon, Ahlav, Akziv, Helbah, Afek, or Rehov,
\v 32 So Asher’s descendants lived with the Canaanites who \add still\add* lived in the region because they didn’t drive them out.
\p
\v 33 Naphtali’s \add descendants\add* didn’t drive out those who were living in Beyt-Shemesh or Beyt-Anat, so they continued to live among them and were forced to work as slaves.
\p
\v 34 The Amorites forced Dan’s descendants to live in the hill country, because they didn’t allow them to come down to the plains.
\v 35 The Amorites were determined to live at Mt. Heres, in Ayalon and Sha’alvim (but when Joseph’s descendants became stronger, they forced the Amorites to work as slaves).
\v 36 The Amorites border went from the Scorpion Pass through Sela and upward from there.
\c 2
\s1 God’s messenger comes to Bokim
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh’s messenger went from Gilgal to Bokim and told \add the Israelites\add*, “I brought you all out of Egypt and led you here to this land that I promised to your ancestors. I had said that I wouldn’t ever break my agreement with you all,
\v 2 but that you mustn’t form treaties with the peoples who lived here, rather you must tear down their altars. But you all haven’t obeyed what I said. What have you \add gone and\add* done?
\v 3 So I’m telling you that I won’t drive your enemies out ahead of you, but they’ll be like thorns in your sides and you’ll all end up being enslaved to their idols.”
\v 4 When Yahweh’s messenger finished saying that, the people wept loudly,
\v 5 so they called that place Bokim \add (which means ‘weeping’)\add* and they offered sacrifices to Yahweh there.
\s1 The death of Joshua
\p
\v 6 Then Joshua dismissed the people and the tribes went to their various regions to take possession of the land.
\v 7 The people obeyed Yahweh as long as Joshua and the elders who had seen the great things that Yahweh had done for Israel , were alive.
\v 8 Then Yahweh’s servant Joshua (son of Nun) died at the age of 110
\v 9 and they buried him in his allocated land at Timnat-Heres, inside Ephraim’s hill country territory north of Mt. Ga’ash.
\s1 Israel turns from Yahweh
\p
\v 10 When all of that generation had passed away, the next generation didn’t know Yahweh or \add even\add* know about what he’d done for the Israelites.
\v 11 They did things that Yahweh considered evil and worshipped Baal \add idols\add*
\v 12 and abandoned the god of their ancestors, Yahweh, the one who brought them out of Egypt. \add Instead\add* they went after other gods—the gods of the people around them. They worshipped them and that made Yahweh angry.
\v 13 They abandoned Yahweh and worshipped the Baal and Ashtarot \add idols\add*.
\v 14 So Yahweh got angry with Israel so he caused raiders to come and take their property and he allowed their enemies to become more powerful so they could no longer stand against them.
\v 15 Whenever the Israelites went into battle, Yahweh worked against them just like he’d said he would and they became very distressed.
\p
\v 16 Then Yahweh gave them leaders\f + \fr 2:16 \ft Traditionally called ‘judges’ in most English translations, but that modern term doesn’t fit their various functions in this account at all well. (‘Leaders’ isn’t perfect either but it’s generic enough to cover most of their roles as guides, heroes, and sometime judges.)\f* to save them from the raiders.
\v 17 However they wouldn’t listen to \add those\add* leaders \add either\add*—continuing to prostitute themselves to other gods and bowing down to idols. They rapidly turned away from their ancestors’ ways and showed no interest in obeying Yahweh’s instructions.
\v 18 Whenever Yahweh gave them a leader, he would support that leader and would save them from their enemies during that leader’s life, because he pitied the people when they groaned from being tormented and oppressed.
\v 19 But then at the death of that leader, they would turn back and behave more corruptly than their ancestors—following other gods and bowing down to idols. They didn’t stop any of their evil activities or their shameless behaviour
\v 20 so Yahweh was very angry at Israel and said, “This nation has broken the agreement that I made with their ancestors and haven’t done what I told them to.
\v 21 I won’t keep driving the people groups away that \add remained\add* when Joshua died,
\v 22 so as to use them to test Israel \add to see\add* whether the Israelites will obey my instructions like their ancestors did, or not.
\v 23 So Yahweh allowed those people groups to stay on, and hence they hadn’t been handed over to Joshua.
\c 3
\s1 The remaining kingdoms in Canaan
\p
\v 1 The \add following\add* are the kingdoms which Yahweh allowed to remain so as to test the \add newer generations\add* of Israel who hadn’t experienced the battles in Canaan
\v 2 (\add if\add* only for the sake of teaching warfare to those generations who hadn’t experienced it):
\v 3 the five Philistine rulers and the Canaanites, the Sidonians, the Hivim that live on Mt. Lebanon between Mt. Baal-Hermon and Levo-Hamat.\f + \fr 3:3 \ft Or, ‘the entrance to Hamat’.\f*
\v 4 They were \add left\add* to test Israel to find out if they would obey Yahweh’s instructions that had been given through Moses to their ancestors,
\v 5 and \add so\add* the Israelites lived among the Canaanites, the Hittites, the Amorites, the Perizim, the Hivim, and the Jebusites.
\v 6 They took their daughters as wives, and gave their own daughters \add to be married\add* to their sons, and they worshipped their gods.
\s1 Otniel
\p
\v 7 So the Israelites did things considered evil by Yahweh and they forgot about their god Yahweh, instead serving the Baal and Asherot \add idols\add*.
\v 8 \add Because of that,\add* Yahweh was very angry with Israel, so he handed them over to King Kushan-Rish’atayim of Aram-Naharayim. The Israelites served him for eight years
\v 9 but when they cried out to Yahweh, he sent them a leader to rescue them: Otniel, son of Caleb’s younger brother Kenaz.
\v 10 Yahweh’s spirit empowered him and he led Israel. He led them into battle against King Kushan-Rish’atayim of Syria, and Yahweh helped them defeat him.
\v 11 So the region had peace for forty years, and then Otniel (Kenaz’s son) died.
\s1 Ehud
\p
\v 12 \add Once\add* again the Israelites started doing things considered evil by Yahweh, so because of that, he strengthened King Eglon of Moab against Israel.
\v 13 King Eglon enlisted warriors from the Ammonites and the Amalekites, and they attacked Israel and captured \add Jericho\add*, the city of palm trees,
\v 14 so the Israelites served him for eighteen years.
\p
\v 15 But the Israelites cried out to Yahweh, he sent them a leader to rescue them: the left-hander Ehud, son of Gera the Benyamite. The Israelites sent him to carry the tribute payment to King Eglon of Moab.
\v 16 Now Ehud had made himself a double-edged sword—a short one about half a metre long—and he strapped it to his right thigh beneath his clothes.
\v 17 Then he presented the tribute to King Eglon of Moab, who was a very fat man,
\v 18 and after leaving \add for home\add*, he sent off the people who’d \add actually\add* carried it \add all\add*.
\v 19 Then from the stone quarries \add that they’d reached\add* near Gilgal, he returned and told the king that he had a private message for him. The king told his attendants to be quiet then sent them out of the room.
\p
\v 20 So Ehud approached where the king was sitting alone in the coolness of his upper room and told him, “I have a message for you from God.” The king stood up from his seat
\v 21 and then Ehud reached with his left hand and pulled the sword from his right thigh and plunged it into the king’s belly.
\v 22 Even the handle went in after the blade and the fat closed up over the handle because Ehud didn’t pull the sword out of his belly. Then he went out to the passage\f + \fr 3:22 \ft The meaning of the last word in the Hebrew here is uncertain, so other translations might differ.\f*
\v 23 and closed and locked the rooms of the upper room and exited through the porch.\f + \fr 3:23 \ft Again, the Hebrew meaning of the last word here is uncertain, so other translations might differ.\f*
\v 24 After he’d left, the servants came and found the doors locked, so they thought the king must be relieving himself in there.
\v 25 They waited until they became worried that he hadn’t opened the doors, so they got a key and opened \add them\add*, only to find their master lying dead on the floor.
\p
\v 26 While the servants had been waiting, Ehud had escaped and passed beyond the quarries, then fleeing to Se’irah.
\v 27 When he got there, he blew a trumpet throughout the Efrati hill country, and \add so\add* the Israelites assembled under his direction down \add in the valley\add*.
\v 28 “Follow after me,” he told them, “because Yahweh will enable you all to defeat your enemies, the Moabites.” So they followed him down to the Jordan \add river\add* and captured the fords opposite Moab, thus preventing anyone from crossing.
\v 29 Then they attacked Moab and killed around ten thousand men—all strong and capable yet none of them escaped.
\v 30 So Moab was subdued that day by the Israelites, and there was peace in the region for eighty years.
\s1 Shamgar
\p
\v 31 After Ehud came Shamgar (Anat’s son), and he rescued Israel by killing six hundred Philistines with a cattle prod.
\c 4
\s1 Deborah and Barak
\p
\v 1 After Ehud died, the Israelites again started doing things considered evil by Yahweh,
\v 2 so he handed them over to King Yavin of Canaan who reigned in Hatsor. Sisera was the commander of his army, and he lived in Haroshet-Hagoyim.
\v 3 The Israelites cried out to Yahweh because Sisera had nine hundred chariots with iron on them, and he cruelly oppressed them for twenty years.
\p
\v 4 Now the prophetess Deborah (Lapidot’s wife) was leading Israel at that time.
\v 5 She would sit under the palm tree between Ramah and Bethel (, in the Efrati hill country), and people would come to her to have their disputes settled.
\v 6 \add One day\add* she summoned Barak (Avinoam’s son from Kedesh in Naphtali and told him, “Israel’s god, Yahweh commands you to draft ten thousand men from Naphtali and Zebulun and \add assemble\add* at Mt. Tavor.
\v 7 Yahweh will lure \add King\add* Yavin’s army commander, Sisera bring his warriors and chariots to the Kishon river, and Yahweh will enable you to defeat them there.”
\p
\v 8 “I’ll go if you’ll come with me,” replied Barak, “but if you won’t come with me, I won’t go.”
\p
\v 9 “I’ll certainly go with you,” she said, “but it won’t be you that’s honoured afterwards, but \add rather\add* Yahweh will use a woman to defeat Sisera.” So Deborah accompanied Barak to Kedesh.
\v 10 There he summoned \add warriors from\add* Zebulun and Naphtali and ten thousand men came, and \add they all went together\add* with Deborah \add to Mt. Tavor\add*.
\p
\v 11 Now Hever had moved \add with his wife Jael and their family\add* away from the other Keynim (and from Moses’ father-in-law Hovav’s descendants) and set up his tent by the oak tree at Tsa’ananim near Kedesh.
\p
\v 12 When Sisera was told that Barak (Avinoam’s son) had gone to Mt. Tavor,
\v 13 he assembled his warriors and their nine hundred chariots (with iron on them) \add and went from\add* Haroshet-Hagoyim to the Kishon riverbed.
\p
\v 14 “Get going,” Deborah told Barak, “because it’s today that Yahweh has gone ahead of you and will help you defeat Sisera.” So Barak led his ten thousand warriors down from Mt. Tavor.
\v 15 Then Yahweh caused Sisera and all his warriors and chariots to be confused as Barak advanced, so Sisera jumped down from his chariot and took off on foot.
\v 16 Barak pursued the men and chariots as far as Haroshet-Hagoyim and they killed them all—not a single one survived.
\p
\v 17 But Sisera had fled on foot \add and ran to\add* Jael’s tent (Hever the Keyni’s wife) because there was peace between King Yavin of Hatsor and the house of Hever the Keyni.
\v 18 Jael went out to meet Sisera and told him, “Stop and rest, my master. Stop and rest here. Don’t be afraid.” So he turned and went into her tent, and she covered him with a blanket.
\p
\v 19 “I’m thirsty,” he said. “Could you get me a little water to drink.” Jael opened a goatskin container and gave him milk to drink, then she covered him \add again\add*.
\v 20 “Stand at the tent doorway,” he said, “and if anyone comes and asks you if you’ve seen a man, say, ‘No.’ ”
\p
\v 21 Then Hever’s wife Jael got a hammer and a tent peg and crept in silently, driving the peg through his temple and pounding it into the ground,. He’d been tired and was sleeping soundly, and it killed him.
\v 22 Meanwhile Barak had been searching for Sisera, and Jael went out to meet him. “Come in here,” she told him, “and I’ll show you the man you’ve been searching for.” He followed her into the tent and wow—Sisera was lying there dead with the peg \add still\add* through his temple.
\p
\v 23 So that day God defeated Canaanite King Yavin’s \add army\add* in front of the Israelites,
\v 24 and as they became stronger against him, they destroyed King Yavin of Canaan.
\c 5
\s1 Deborah and Barak sing together
\p
\v 1 That day, Deborah and Barak sang this song:
\q1
\v 2 “When the leaders take the lead in Israel,
\q1 when the people offer themselves willingly,
\q1 Yahweh be blessed.
\q1
\v 3 Listen you kings.
\q1 Pay attention you rulers.
\q1 I will sing to Yahweh, yes, even me.
\q1 I will make music for Israel’s god Yahweh.
\q1
\v 4 Yahweh, you left Se’ir.
\q1 You marched out of the Edom region.
\q2 The earth quaked.
\q2 The heavens also dropped.
\q1 And the clouds poured down rain.
\q1
\v 5 Mountains melted in Yahweh’s presence,
\q1 Yes, Mt. Sinai shook in front of Israel’s god, Yahweh.
\b
\q1
\v 6 In the days of Anat’s son Shamgar,
\q1 in the days of Jael,
\q1 \add main\add* roads were abandoned,
\q1 and travellers walked twisting trails.
\q1
\v 7 Israelite villages were abandoned.
\q1 No one lived there until I, Deborah, became their leader.
\q1 I became one of Israel’s mothers.
\q1
\v 8 When Israel chose new gods,
\q2 →then enemies attacked the \add city\add* gates.
\q1 No shield or spear could be found
\q2 among forty thousand in Israel.
\q1
\v 9 I’m thankful to Israel’s commanders
\q2 who volunteered \add to help\add* the people.
\q1 Yahweh be blessed.
\q1
\v 10 You riders of white donkeys,
\q1 you who sit on saddle blankets,
\q1 you who are walking on the road,
\q2 tell about \add this\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 11 From the sounds of conflict over the watering places
\q2 they recount Yahweh’s righteousness
\q2 and about his righteous warriors in Israel.
\b
\q1 Then Yahweh’s people went down to the \add city\add* gates.
\b
\q1
\v 12 Wake up, Deborah, wake up.
\q1 Wake up, wake up, sing a song.
\q1 Barak, get moving and capture your prisoners,
\q2 you son of Avinoam.
\q1
\v 13 Then the survivor went down to the leading citizens.
\q1 Yahweh’s people went down to the mighty. ???
\q1
\v 14 From Ephraim their root was with Amalek.
\q1 After you, Benjamin, among your people.
\q1 From Makir \add (in Manasseh’s\add* \add tribe)\add*, the commanders came down.
\q1 And from Zebulun, those who march carrying an officer’s staff.
\q1
\v 15 My princes in Issachar were with Deborah.
\q1 And Issachar was also with Barak.
\q1 He was sent into a valley on his heels.
\q1 There was intense heart searching among the clans of Reuben.
\q1
\v 16 Why did you sit between the sheep pens?
\q1 To hear the whistling for the flocks?
\q1 There was intense heart searching among the clans of Reuben.
\q1
\v 17 Gilead settled east of the Jordan.
\q1 But, Dan, why does he stay on ships?
\q1 Asher stayed at the sea coast
\q1 and lives by his jetties.
\q1
\v 18 Zebulun were a tribe that \add devotedly\add* risked losing their lives.
\q1 And Naphtali was at their heights on the battlefield.
\b
\q1
\v 19 Kings came and made war.
\q1 Then the Canaanite kings fought at Ta’anak,
\q2 at the Megiddo springs.
\q1 ^\add But\add* they weren’t able to plunder any silver.
\q1
\v 20 From the sky, the stars battled.
\q1 From their courses, they fought against Sisera.
\q1
\v 21 The Kishon river swept them away.
\q1 An ancient river that Kishon river.
\q1 March on with strength, my soul.
\q1
\v 22 Then the horses’ hooves struck.
\q1 His galloping stallions charging.
\b
\q1
\v 23 ‘Curse \add that\add* Meroz \add place\add*,’ said Yahweh’s messenger.
\q1 ‘You should certainly curse its inhabitants
\q1 because they didn’t come and help Yahweh—
\q1 to help Yahweh against the mighty ones.’
\b
\q1
\v 24 Jael will be an honoured woman.
\q2 The wife of Hever the Keyni.
\q1 She deserves the most praise out of \add all\add* women who live in tents.
\q1
\v 25 He asked for water—she gave him milk,
\q2 Yogurt in a bowl suitable for the leading citizens.
\q1
\v 26 Her hands reached out for the tent peg,
\q2 and her right hand for the workman’s hammer.
\q1 She hammered Sisera—shattering his head—
\q2 shattering and piercing his temple.
\q1
\v 27 He bowed down between her feet—he fell—he lay down.
\q1 He bowed down between her feet—where he bowed down, there he fell—destroyed.
\b
\q1
\v 28 Sisera’s mother looked out the window.
\q1 She peered through the lattice, crying out,
\q2 ‘Why is his chariot so slow to return?
\q2 Why are the hoofbeats of his chariots delayed?’
\q1
\v 29 Her wise ladies answered her.
\q1 Indeed, she keeps telling herself
\q1
\v 30 ‘Won’t they be gathering and dividing the plunder?
\q1 A woman or two for each warrior \add to bear children\add*.
\q1 Finding some colourful material for Sisera.
\q1 Taking some embroidered fabrics.
\q1 Dyed scarves as plunder.’
\b
\q1
\v 31 May all your enemies perish like that, Yahweh.
\q1 But may those who love you shine like the sun rising in its strength.”
\m Then that region had peace for forty years.
\c 6
\s1 Gideon
\p
\v 1 The Israelites did things that Yahweh considered evil so he handed them over to the Midian for seven years
\v 2 and the Midianites oppressed them badly. It was so bad that the Israelites made dens for themselves up in the hills, in caves, and in small, fortified settlements.
\v 3 Whenever the Israelites planted a crop, the Midianites or the Amalekites, or eastern groups would attack them.
\v 4 They would set up their camps around them and destroy the crops as far away as Gaza—they left nothing for the Israelites to eat, and no sheep, cattle, or donkeys.
\v 5 It was like a swarm of locusts when they’d \add all\add* come with their tents and their livestock—you couldn’t even count their camels! They entered the region to totally ravage it.
\v 6 Thus Israel was severely weakened by Midian, and the Israelites cried out to Yahweh.
\p
\v 7 When the Israelites cried out to Yahweh concerning Midian,
\v 8 Yahweh sent a prophet to them and he told them, “Israel’s god Yahweh says, ‘I myself brought you all out of slavery in Egypt—
\v 9 rescuing you from the Egyptians who were oppressing you all. I drove them out when you all entered and I’ve certainly given their land to you.
\v 10 I told you all that I’m your god Yahweh and that when you live in the Amorite regions, you all mustn’t worship their gods. But you didn’t listen to what I said.’ ”
\p
\v 11 \add One day\add* Yahweh’s messenger came and sat beneath the oak tree in Ofrah \add town\add*. (The tree belonged to the Aviezri, Yoash.) Yoah’s son Gideon was beating wheat \add down\add* in a wine-pressing \add pit\add* to hide it from the Midianites
\v 12 when Yahweh’s messenger appeared to him and said, “Yahweh is with you, you powerful warrior.”
\p
\v 13 “With me, my master?” Gideon asked. “If Yahweh is with us, then why’s all of this happened to us? Where are all of the miracles that our ancestors told us about, saying, ‘Didn’t Yahweh bring us up out of Egypt?’ But now Yahweh has abandoned us and handed us over to the Midianites.”
\p
\v 14 Then Yahweh turned to him and said, “Use your strength to set Israel free from the grasp of Midian. Haven’t I sent you \add to do that\add*?”
\p
\v 15 “Please, my master,” Gideon replied, “how could \em I\em* save Israel? Look, my clan is the weakest in Manasseh and \add on top of that\add*, I’m the youngest in my family.”
\p
\v 16 “I’ll help you,” said Yahweh, “and you’ll strike down all the Midianites as \add easily as battling just\add* one man.”
\p
\v 17 “If indeed I’ve found favour in your eyes,” Gideon requested, “then show me a miracle so I’ll know that it’s God speaking to me.
\v 18 Please wait here \add while I go and prepare\add* my gift for you and bring it back here for you.”
\p “\add Okay,\add* I’ll stay \add here\add* until you get back,” he said.
\p
\v 19 Then Gideon went a prepared a young goat and unrisen bread. He put the meat in a basket and the soup in a pot and brought them out to the oak tree and offered them to him.
\v 20 Then God’s messenger said, “Take the meat and the flat bread and put them on this rock, then pour the soup out \add over it\add*.” So Gideon did that
\v 21 and Yahweh’s messenger stretched out the staff that he was holding and touched the meat and bread with the end of it. A fire flamed up from the rock and burnt up the meat and bread, then Yahweh’s messenger went out of his sight.\f + \fr 6:21 \ft It’s unclear from the text, whether he just got up and walked away, or if he vanished in a more miraculous fashion.\f*
\p
\v 22 When Gideon realised that it \add really\add* had been Yahweh’s messenger, he was upset and said, “Oh no, my master Yahweh, what will happen now that I’ve seen Yahweh’s face to face?”
\v 23 But Yahweh replied to him, “Peace to you. Don’t be afraid—you won’t die.”
\v 24 So Gideon built an altar there to \add sacrifice\add* to Yahweh and named it ‘Yahweh is peace’. (It still stands there at Ofrah of the Aviezri to this day.)
\p
\v 25 That same night, Yahweh told him, “Take the young bull that belongs to your father, and the second bull seven years old. Break the altar of Baal into pieces that belongs to your father, and cut down the Asherah pole that’s beside it.
\v 26 Build a regular altar to your god Yahweh on top of this fortress, then take the second bull and offer a whole burnt offering using the Asherah pole that you cut down for firewood.”
\v 27 So Gideon took ten of his servants and did what Yahweh had instructed him to, but he did it at night because he was afraid to do it in the daytime—\add fearing the anger of\add* his father’s household and the men of the city.
\p
\v 28 When the men of the city got up early the next morning, wow, the Baal’s altar had been torn down, the Asherah pole that was beside it had been cut down, and the second bull had been offered on a \add new\add* altar that had been built.
\v 29 So people started asking each other, “Who did this?” After some investigation, they said, “Yoash’s son Gideon did it.”
\v 30 Then the men of the city told Yoash, “Bring your son out \add here\add* to be executed, because he tore down the Baal’s altar and cut down the Asherah \add pole\add* that was beside it.”
\p
\v 31 “Are you all really fighting Baal’s battle for him?” Yoash replied to those standing against him. “Are you all really helping him? Anyone who fights on his behalf should be put to death while it is still the morning. If Baal is a god, then let him stand up for himself when someone tears down his altar.”
\v 32 So after that, he called Gideon ‘Yeruba’al’ to say ‘Let Baal defend himself,’ because he had torn down Baal’s altar.
\p
\v 33 Meanwhile all the Midianite and Amalekite \add warriors\add* and others from the east, assembled together. Then they crossed the Jordan and set up camp in the Jezreel valley.
\v 34 But Yahweh’s spirit enabled Gideon, and he blew a trumpet and called the \add men of\add* Aviezer to follow him.
\v 35 Then he sent messengers throughout all of \add the regions of\add* Manasseh, Asher, Zebulun, and Naphtali, and summoned \add their warriors\add* to come and join with them.
\p
\v 36 Then Gideon said to God, “If it’s \add really\add* you rescuing Israel through me like you said,
\v 37 I’ll put this woollen fleece on the threshing floor \add tonight\add*. If there’s dew on the fleece in the morning, but it’s dry around it, then I’ll know that you’ll rescue Israel through me just as you said.”
\v 38 And that’s what happened—he rose early the next morning and squeezed the fleece and wrung out a bowl full of water from the dew.
\v 39 Then Gideon said to God, “Don’t be angry with me, but can I ask one more thing. Let me put the fleece out this time, and let the fleece be dry, but wet all around it with dew.”
\v 40 So God did it that night, and \add the next morning\add* only the fleece was dry, but everywhere around was covered with dew.
\c 7
\s1 Gideon defeats the Midianites
\p
\v 1 Then Gideon (\add also known as\add* Yeruba’al) and the warriors with him got up early and \add went to\add* Harod spring. The Midianite camp was north of them in the valley below the Moreh hill.
\p
\v 2 Then Yahweh told Gideon, “You’ve got too many warriors with you for me to give victory over the Midianites, because what if Israel takes the glory for themselves thinking that their own strength saved them.
\v 3 So tell the men that anyone who’s afraid or trembling can leave Mt. Gilead and go back home.” Twenty-two thousand of them went back, leaving \add only\add* ten thousand.
\p
\v 4 “That’s still too many,” Yahweh told Gideon. “Bring them down to the spring and I’ll short-list them there. Anyone I tell you to go with you, can do so, but anyone that I say won’t go with you, won’t go.”
\v 5 So he took them down to the water and Yahweh told him, “Separate out those who lap up the water with their tongues like a dog would, along with those who kneel down to drink.”
\v 6 It turned out that \add only\add* three hundred men used their hands to bring water to their mouths, and all the rest knelt down to drink the water.
\v 7 “I’ll rescue you all from Midian with the three hundred men who lapped the water \add from their hands\add*,” Yahweh told Gideon. “Let all the others go back to their homes.”
\v 8 Then the three-hundred warriors collected the food and ram’s horns from the others before Gideon sent the others back to their own homes.
\p The Midian camp was below them \add down\add* in the valley
\v 9 and during the night Yahweh told Gideon, “Get ready and \add lead them\add* down into the camp, because I have made you victorious.
\v 10 But if you’re afraid to attack, take your servant Purah down to their camp
\v 11 and you’ll hear what they’re saying, then you’ll have the courage to go down \add and attack\add* the camp.” So Gideon and his servant Purah went \add quietly\add* down to the unit at the edge of the camp.
\p
\v 12 The Midianites and Amalekites and others from the east covered the valley like a swarm of locusts. \add Even\add* their camels were as numerous as sand grains on the beach.
\v 13 When Gideon got close, a man was telling his companion about his dream. “Listen,” he said, “I had a dream and wow, a round loaf of barley bread was tumbling into our camp. It came as far as the tent and struck it so that it fell and turned it upside down, and collapsed \add completely\add*.”
\p
\v 14 “That could only be the sword of Yoash’s son Gideon from Israel,” the other man replied. “The true God has caused Midian and all of the camp to be defeated by him.”
\p
\v 15 When Gideon heard the account of the dream and its interpretation, he thanked God, then they returned to their camp and commanded, “Get ready to go because Yahweh has given you all victory over the Midianite camp.”
\v 16 Then he divided the three hundred men into three units, and gave each of them rams’ horns and \add clay\add* jars with \add burning\add* torches inside them,
\v 17 and instructed them, “Watch me and do what I do. See, when I get to the edge of their camp, do the same as me.
\v 18 When I blow the ram’s horn trumpet, all of you with horns should blow them and \add everyone\add* will shout, ‘For Yahweh and for Gideon!’ ”
\p
\v 19 When Gideon and the hundred men with him got to the edge of the camp, it was the beginning of the middle nightwatch and the fresh guards had just gotten into their places. Gideon and his unit blew their trumpets and smashed the jars that they were carrying.
\v 20 Then \add all\add* three units blew the trumpets and smashed their jars. They held the torches in their left hands and the trumpets in their right hands, and they shouted out, “A sword for Yahweh and for Gideon.”
\v 21 Then everyone stood in their place around the camp, but the \add Midianite\add* army cried out \add in alarm\add* and took off.
\v 22 When the three hundred ram’s horns sounded, Yahweh caused the Midianite army to start fighting each other with their swords, and the army fled as far as Beyt-Hashitah (towards Tsererah) and towards the border of Abel-Meholah (towards Tabat).
\p
\v 23 Then the warriors from Naphtali, Asher, and from all of Manasseh were summoned to help with pursuing the Midianites.
\v 24 Gideon \add also\add* sent messengers through Ephraim’s hill country to tell them, “Go down \add to the Jordan\add* \add river\add* opposite Midian and take control of the crossings to capture them between as far as Beyt-Barah.” So all the men of Ephraim were summoned and they commandeered the Jordan crossings as far as Beyt-Barah.
\v 25 They chased after the Midianites and captured their two princes, Orev and Ze’ev. They killed Orev at the rock \add now called\add* ‘Orev’s rock’, and they killed Ze’ev in a wine-pressing \add pit\add* \add now called\add* ‘Ze’ev’s wine-press’, and they brought their heads back across the Jordan to Gideon.
\c 8
\s1 The final defeat of the Midianites
\p
\v 1 \add But\add* then the Efrati men challenged Gideon, “Why have you side-tracked us? Why didn’t you call us when you went to battle against the Midianites?” And they severely rebuked him.
\p
\v 2 “What have I achieved compared to you guys?” answered Gideon. “Aren’t the seconds of Ephraim’s grapes better than the \add whole\add* grape harvest of Aviezer?
\v 3 God helped you all defeat Orev and Ze’ev, the princes of Midian. What did I do compared to that?” After he said that, they \add all\add* calmed down again.
\p
\v 4 Then Gideon and his three hundred warriors crossed the Jordan \add going east\add*. They were all exhausted, but continued the pursuit.
\v 5 \add When they arrived at the town\add* of Sukot, Gideon asked the leaders, “Please give my men some bread to eat because they’re exhausted, but we’re chasing Zevah and Tsalmuna, the Midianite kings.”
\p
\v 6 However, the Sukot officials asked \add cautiously\add*, “Do you already have Zevah and Tsalmuna in your grasp that we should give food to \em your\em* army?”
\p
\v 7 “Let me assure you all,” Gideon responded, “that when Yahweh has helped us capture Zevah and Tsalmuna, I will rip off your flesh with the wilderness thorns and briers.”
\p
\v 8 From there they climbed up to Penuel, and he asked them for food in a similar manner, and the leaders there responded similarly to the leaders of Sukot.
\v 9 So he said to the men of Penuel as well, “When I return safely, I’ll tear down this tower.”
\p
\v 10 Now Zevah and Tsalmuna were in Karkor with around fifteen thousand warriors—all that remained out of all of that allied Midianite camp of eastern people, because one hundred and twenty thousand of their warriors had already been killed.
\v 11 Gideon approached them \add unexpectedly\add* via the caravan route east of Novah and Yagebehah, and struck their camp when they were feeling secure.
\v 12 Zevah and Tsalmuna fled, but they chased after those two Midianite kings and captured them, thus defeating their entire army.
\p
\v 13 The Gideon (Yoash’s son) \add and his warriors\add* returned from battle, going through the Heres pass.
\v 14 He captured a young man from Sukot and questioned him—writing down the names of the seventy-seven leaders and elders of Sukot.
\v 15 Then he returned to Sukot and told them, “Look \add here\add* at Zevah and Tsalmuna—the ones you taunted me about when you asked, ‘Do you already have Zevah and Tsalmuna in your grasp that we should give food to your weary men.’ ”
\v 16 He took the city elders and \add had them punished\add* with wilderness thorns and briers to teach them a lesson,
\v 17 then \add they went to\add* Penuel and tore down the tower and executed the city leaders.
\p
\v 18 Then Gideon asked Zevah and Tsalmuna, “What were the men like that you killed at Tavor?”
\p “They were just like you,” they replied. “They seemed like they could have been a king’s sons.”
\p
\v 19 “They were my brothers—the sons of my mother,” he said. “As Yahweh lives, if only you had let them live, I wouldn’t have killed you.”
\v 20 Then he ordered Yeter, his oldest son, “Go and kill them.” But Yeter didn’t pull out his sword because he was afraid—he was still a youth.
\p
\v 21 Then Zevah and Tsalmuna said, “Come and kill us yourself—be a man and use your own strength.” So Gideon went over and killed Zevah and Tsalmuna, and then he took the crescent-shaped ornaments that were on their camels’ necks.
\p
\v 22 Then \add a group of\add* Israelite men said to Gideon, “We want you and your sons and your future descendants to rule over us, because you have saved us from the Midianites.”
\p
\v 23 But Gideon answered, “\add No,\add* I won’t rule over you myself, nor will my son rule over you. Yahweh will rule over you.”
\v 24 However, he continued, “Let me ask you all for something: that each man give me the earrings from his spoil.” (The enemies had gold earrings because they were Ishmaelites.)
\p
\v 25 “We can certainly do that for you,” they replied. So they spread out a garment and each man threw the earrings from his spoil onto it.
\v 26 Now the weight of the gold earrings of gold came to twenty kilograms, and then there were the crescent-shaped ornaments, the pendants, the dyed purple garments that the Midianite kings had been wearing, as well as the collars that had been around their camels’ necks.
\v 27 Then Gideon made the gold into an idol\f + \fr 8:27 \ft We don’t really know what ‘efod’ means in this context. (It was also used to refer to priestly clothing.)\f* and placed it in his \add home\add* city of Ofrah, and the Israelites prostituted themselves to it there, and so it became like a trap for Gideon and his household.
\p
\v 28 So Midian was kept under control by the Israelites and they didn’t continue to oppress the people, and there was peace in the region for forty years during Gideon’s time.
\s1 The death of Gideon
\p
\v 29 Then \add Gideon\add* (Yoash’s son, \add also known as\add* Yeruba’al) went back and lived in his house,
\v 30 and he fathered seventy sons because he had many wives.
\v 31 He \add also\add* had a mistress in Shechem—she also bore him a son that he named Avimelek.
\v 32 Gideon died at a good old age and was buried in the tomb of Yoash his father, at Ofrah of the Abiezerites.
\p
\v 33 However after Gideon had died, the Israelites turned away again \add from Yahweh\add* and prostituted themselves to the Baals—making Baal-Berit for themselves as a god.
\v 34 They forgot about their god Yahweh—the one who had rescued them from the clutch of all of their enemies all around.
\v 35 They didn’t show lasting loyalty to Gideon’s family, despite all the good that he’d done within Israel.
\c 9
\s1 Avimelek snatches the kingship
\p
\v 1 Now Gideon’s son Avimelek went to Shechem to his mother’s brothers and spoke to those uncles and to all the extended family of his mother’s father, saying,
\v 2 “Please ask around all the citizens of Shechem: ‘What is better for you: Gideon’s seventy sons ruling over you, or having a single man in charge?’ Remember that I’m your flesh and bone.”
\v 3 So his uncles working on his behalf, passed that onto all the citizens of Shechem, and they became convinced in Avimelek, because they said, “He’s our brother.”
\v 4 Then they gave him seventy silver coins from the temple of Baal-Berit, and Avimelek used them to entice some rough lay-abouts to work for him.
\v 5 Then they went to his father’s house at Ofrah, and he killed his seventy \add half-\add*brothers (Gideon’s sons) there on one stone. But Gideon’s youngest son Jotham survived because he had hidden \add from them\add*.
\v 6 Then all of the \add prominent\add* citizens of Shechem and all of Beyt-Milo assembled themselves, and they went and installed Avimelek as king beside an oak tree near the standing pillar\f + \fr 9:6 \ft Likely connected with idol worship.\f* that was in Shechem.
\s1 Jotham’s tree parable
\p
\v 7 When they told Jotham, he went and stood on the top of Mt. Gerizim and shouted out to them all in a loud voice, “Listen to me, citizens of Shechem, so God will listen to all of you.
\v 8 Once the trees went about in order to appoint a king for themselves, So they said to the \em olive tree\em*, ‘Reign over us.’
\v 9 But the olive tree replied to them, ‘Have I stopped producing my valuable oil used to honour gods and men, that I should go to sway above the \add other\add* trees?’
\v 10 So the trees said to the \em fig tree\em*, ‘You come \add and\add* reign over us.’
\v 11 But the fig tree \add answered\add* them, ‘Have I stopped producing my sweetness and my good fruit, that I should go to sway above the \add other\add* trees?’
\v 12 Then the trees said to the grapevine, ‘You come \add and\add* reign over us.’
\v 13 But the vine replied, ‘Have I stopped producing my new wine, which cheers gods and man, that I should go to sway over the \add other\add* trees?’
\v 14 Then all the trees said to the thornbush, ‘\em You\em* come \add and\add* reign over us!’
\v 15 The thornbush told the trees, ‘If you all are really wanted to anoint me as king over you, come \add and\add* take refuge in \em my\em* shade. But if you all don’t, may the thornbush send out fire to burn up Lebanon’s cedar \add forests\add*.’
\p
\v 16 “Therefore did you all act with honesty and integrity when you installed Avimelek as king? Did you all treat Gideon’s family well, and did you all repay him appropriately for what he did for you all?
\v 17 My father fought on your behalf, and even risked his life out front when he rescued you all from the Midianites,
\v 18 but today you’ve all risen up against my father’s family and killed his sons—seventy men on one stone. Then you’ve installed Avimelek, the son of his female servant, as king over the citizens of Shechem because he’s your brother.
\v 19 Now if you’ve all acted with honesty and integrity with towards Gideon and his family today, be happy with Avimelek, and he’ll also be happy with all of you,
\v 20 but if not, then fire will come out of Avimelek and destroy the citizens of Shechem and Beyt-Milo. Fire will also come out from Shechem’s citizens and from Beyt-Milo, and it’ll destroy Avimelek.”
\v 21 Then Jotham fled away and escaped and went to Be’er and settled there away from the presence of his \add half-\add*brother Avimelek.
\p
\v 22 Avimelek ruled over Israel for three years,
\v 23 then God sent an evil spirit between him and the citizens of Shechem, so the citizens there dealt treacherously with Avimelek.
\v 24 The violent treatment of Gideon’s seventy sons kept getting brought up, and by it was all blamed on their brother Avimelek who had killed them, and on \add those of\add* Shechem’s citizens who assisted him in the killing of his brothers.
\v 25 So the Shechem citizens placed men in ambush along the tops of the hills to get him, but they robbed everyone who went passed them along the road, and \add so\add* it was reported to Avimelek.
\p
\v 26 Then Ga’al (Eved’s son) came along with his brothers and moved into Shechem, and the citizens there \add began to\add* trust them.
\v 27 They went out into the field, and they picked grapes from their vineyards and pressed them. They celebrated harvest festivals and they went into the house of their gods, then they ate and drank, and they cursed Avimelek.
\v 28 Then Ga’al (Eved’s son) said, “Who’s Avimelek, and who’s Shechem, that we should serve him? Isn’t Avimelek Gideon’s son, and Zevul is his deputy? Serve the descendants of Shechem’s father Hamor, but why should we serve Avimelek?
\v 29 Who will fight with me to defeat those people and depose Avimelek? Then he said to Avimelek, ‘Gather your army and come and fight.’ ”
\p
\v 30 When Zevul, the prince of the city, heard what Ga’al (Eved’s son) had said, he got very angry
\v 31 and he sent messengers to Avimelek in secret, saying, “Listen, Eved’s son Ga’al and his brothers are coming to Shechem, and wow, they’re inciting the city against you.
\v 32 You and the people who are with you should go out at night and lie in ambush in the countryside.
\v 33 Then when the sun rises in the morning, you all should set out early and spread out in an attack against the city. Keep watch, then when Ga’al and the people with him are coming out toward you \add to fight\add*, then you \add can overpower them and\add* treat him however you think fit.”
\p
\v 34 So Avimelek and all the people with him went out in the night and they set up four units in an ambush against Shechem.
\v 35 \add In the morning,\add* Ga’al (Eved’s son) went out and he stood at the entrance of the city gate. Then Avimelek and his men stood up from their hiding places.
\v 36 When Ga’al saw the people, he said to Zevul, “Look, a group of people is coming down from the tops of the hills!”
\p “You are seeing the shadow of the hills as men,” Zevul responded.
\p
\v 37 But Ga’al said again, “Look, groups of people are coming down from beside the top of the hill, and another group is coming from the way of the ‘oak of the diviners’.”
\p
\v 38 Then Zevul said to him, “Well, you were the one who said, ‘Who is Avimelek that we should serve him?’ Aren’t these the people who you were scorning? Indeed now, go out and fight against them.”
\v 39 So Ga’al went out in front of Shechem’s citizens and fought against Avimelek.
\v 40 Avimelek pursued him, and he had to flee away from him. Many \add of their men\add* fell fatally wounded as far as the entrance of the \add city\add* gate.
\v 41 Then Avimelek settled in Arumah, but Zevul drove Ga’al and his brothers away from living in Shechem.
\p
\v 42 Now it so happened that the next day, the people \add of Shechem\add* went out into the countryside, and it was reported to Avimelek.
\v 43 So he took his men and split them into three units. Then he waited in ambush in the countryside. When he saw and wow, the people were going out of the city, then he attacked and killed them.
\v 44 Avimelek and the units that were with him had spread out and they stood at the entrance of the city gate, and two units spread out against everyone who was in the field and killed them.
\v 45 Avimelek spent that whole day attacking Shechem. He captured the city and they killed the people who were in it. Then he tore it down, and scattered salt on the land.
\p
\v 46 When all the lords of the Shechem tower heard, they went into the citadel of the temple for El-Berit,
\v 47 but it was reported to Avimelek that all the lords of the Shechem tower had gathered there.
\v 48 So Avimelek and all the people with him, went up to Mt. Tsalmon. He took axes and cut a branch down from the trees, then lifted it onto his shoulder and said to the people who were with him, “What I just did, you all quickly do the same.”
\v 49 So all the people also each cut off a branch and then followed Avimelek. They piled them on top of the underground chamber, and that’s how they burnt out the underground chamber. As a result, all the distinguished people in the Shechem tower also died—about a thousand men and women.
\p
\v 50 Then Avimelek went to Tevets and set up camp around the city and captured it.
\v 51 But there was a strong tower in the middle of the city, so all of the men and the women and all the prominent citizens fled to it. Once they were all in, they barricaded it up and they went up onto the tower roof.
\v 52 Then Avimelek \add and his men\add* approached the tower and fought against it, and he came in as close as the tower entrance in order to set fire to it,
\v 53 but a woman threw an upper millstone down onto Avimelek’s head and it crushed his skull.
\v 54 Then he cried out hurriedly to the young man bearing his armour, “Draw out your sword and put me to death, because I don’t want it to be said afterwards that I was killed by a woman.” So his young man stabbed him so that he died.
\v 55 When the Israelite warriors saw that Avimelek was dead, they all returned to their own homes.
\p
\v 56 Thus God avenged the evil of Avimelek that he had done to his father \add Gideon\add* by killing his seventy brothers,
\v 57 and God caused all the evil done by Shechem’s men to return back onto their own heads, and the curse of Gideon’s \add youngest\add* son Jotham, came true on them.
\c 10
\s1 Tola
\p
\v 1 Now after Avimelek, Tola (the son of Puah, the son of Dodo, from the tribe of Issachar came on the scene to rescue Israel. He settled in Shamir, in the Ephraim hill country.
\v 2 He led Israel for twenty-three years, then he died and was buried in Shamir.
\s1 Yair
\p
\v 3 After him, Yair the Giladi led Israel for twenty-two years.
\v 4 He had thirty sons that rode on thirty donkeys. There were thirty cities for them in the Gilead region which they call Yair’s villages to this day.
\v 5 Then Yair died and he was buried in Kamon.
\s1 The making again of bad of Israelites
\p
\v 6 The Israelites continued to do what Yahweh considered evil: they worshipped the Ba’als, the Ashtarot, the gods of Syria, the gods of Sidon, the gods of Moab, the gods of the sons of Amon, and the gods of the Philistines. They forsook Yahweh and didn’t serve him.
\v 7 Yahweh was very angry with Israel, so he allowed the Philistines and the Ammonites to harass them.
\v 8 They shattered and oppressed the Israelites that year, but it went on for eighteen years for all the Israelites who were on the opposite side of the Jordan in the land of the Amorites, which is in the Gilead.
\v 9 Then the Ammonites also crossed over the Jordan to fight against Judah, against Benjamin, and against the half-tribe of Ephraim.
\p That caused incredible distress for the Israelites
\v 10 so they cried out to Yahweh, “We have sinned against you, because we have abandoned our god and we have served the Ba’als.”
\p
\v 11 “Wasn’t it from the Egyptians,” Yahweh responded, “from the Amorites, from the Ammonites, from the Philistines,
\v 12 the Sidonians and Amalek and Maeon, when they oppressed you that you cried out to me, and I rescued you all from them?
\v 13 Yet you’ve all abandoned me and served other gods. Therefore, I won’t not continue to rescue you.
\v 14 Go and cry out to the gods that you’ve all chosen for yourselves. They should deliver you all at the time of your distress.”
\p
\v 15 Then the Israelites said to Yahweh, “We’ve sinned. You do to us according to all that is good in your eyes. Only please rescue us today.”
\v 16 They removed the foreign gods from among them, and they worshipped Yahweh and so he became concerned with Israel’s suffering.
\p
\v 17 Now the Ammonites had assembled and were camped in Gilead. So the Israelites gathered together and they camped at Mitspah.
\v 18 Then the princes of Gilead said to each other, “Who will lead us to fight against the Ammonites? He can become the leader over all of us who live in Gilead.”
\c 11
\s1 Jephthah
\p
\v 1 Now Jephthah the Giladi was a fierce warrior, but he was a prostitute’s son and his father was Gilead.
\v 2 Gilead’s wife also gave birth to sons for him, and \add when\add* those sons of the wife grew up, they drove Jephthah out and told him, “You won’t get any inheritance from our father’s estate because you’re a son of another woman.”
\v 3 So Jephthah fled from the presence of his \add half-\add*brothers and he settled in the Tov region. Unprincipled men associated around Jephthah and went around with him.
\p
\v 4 Some time later, the Ammonites battled against Israel.
\v 5 and that was when the elders of Gilead went to summon Jephthah from the Tov region.
\v 6 Then requested Jephthah, “Come and be our commander so we can fight against the Ammonites.”
\p
\v 7 “Don’t you all hate me? Jephthah asked them. “Didn’t you yourselves drive me out of my father’s house? So why have you all come to me now when you have troubles?”
\p
\v 8 “Well, true,” the Gilead elders replied, “\add But\add* now we’ve turned back to you. So join with us and fight against the Ammonites, and you’ll become commander over all of who lives in Gilead.”
\p
\v 9 “If you bring me back to fight against the Ammonites,” Jephthah asked them, “and if Yahweh helps me defeat them, \add is it correct that\add* I’ll actually become your leader?”
\p
\v 10 “Yahweh will be a witness between us,” the Gilead elders responded, “that we’ll most certainly do what you just said.”
\v 11 So Jephthah went with the Gilead elders, and the people set him as commander and leader over themselves. (Jephthah had spoken all those words before Yahweh at Mitspah.)
\p
\v 12 Then Jephthah sent messengers to the Ammonite king, demanding, “What’s happened with respect to me and to you, that you’ve come against me to fight over my land?”
\p
\v 13 The Ammonite king responded to Jephthah’s messengers, “Because the Israelites seized my land when they came out of Egypt. It went from the Arnon \add river\add* up to the Yabok \add river\add*, and over to the Jordan \add river\add*. Now return the land peaceably.”
\p
\v 14 Then Jephthah sent messengers back to the Ammonite king
\v 15 to tell him, “Jephthah wants you to know that Israel didn’t take land from Moab nor from you Ammonites.
\v 16 However in their coming up from Egypt, Israel went through the wilderness as far as the Red sea until arriving at Kadesh.
\v 17 When Israel sent messengers to the king of Edom, saying, ‘Please may we pass through your land,’ the king of Edom wouldn’t listen. Israel likewise sent to the king of Moab, but he wasn’t willing \add either\add*, so Israel stayed at Kadesh.
\v 18 Then we went through the wilderness and turned away from Edom and Moab, then went \add the long way\add* around the eastern border of Moab. They camped on the other side of the Arnon \add river\add*, but they didn’t go within Moab’s borders, because the Arnon was the border of Moab.
\v 19 Israel sent messengers to the Amorite King Sihon, the king of Heshbon and asked him, ‘Please, let us pass through your land as far as our place.’
\v 20 But \add King\add* Sihon didn’t trust Israel passing through within his border, so he assembled all of his people together and they camped at Yahats, and he battled with Israel.
\v 21 Then Israel’s god, Yahweh, handed \add King\add* Sihon and all his people over to Israel and we defeated them. Thus Israel took possession of all of the land of the Amorites inhabiting that region—
\v 22 everything within the Amorite territory from the Arnon \add river\add* to the Yabok, and from the wilderness as far as the Jordan.
\v 23 So since it was Israel’s god Yahweh that expelled the Amorites out of the presence of his people Israel, do you actually think that you can take it \add now\add*?
\v 24 Wouldn’t you take possession if your god Chemosh, allowed you to? So \add too\add* all that our god Yahweh has dispossessed ahead of us, we’ll possess that.
\v 25 Are you really better now than Tsipor’s son, King Balak of Moab? Did he dare contend with Israel or did he ever wage war against them?
\v 26 Israel lived in Heshbon and in its villages for three hundred years, and in Aroer and in its villages, and in all the cities that are along the banks of the Arnon—so why didn’t you repossess them during that time?
\v 27 I haven’t done anything wrong to you, but you’re doing wrong in dealing with me by fighting against me. Yahweh, the judge, will decide today between the Israelites and the Ammonites.”
\v 28 But the Ammonite king didn’t take any notice of Jephthah’s message to him.
\p
\v 29 Then Yahweh’s spirit empowered Jephthah, and he passed through the Gilead and Manasseh, and through Mitspah of Gilead, and from there he passed through the Ammonite region,
\v 30 and he made a promise to Yahweh, “If you really give me victory over the Ammonites,
\v 31 then whoever’s the first person to come out of my house to meet me when I return in peace from the battle against the Ammonites, that person will belong to Yahweh, and I will offer him up as a whole burnt offering.”
\v 32 So Jephthah went to the Ammonite territory to fight against them, and Yahweh enabled them to defeat them.
\v 33 Then he attacked them from Aroer and as far as the entrance to Minit, twenty cities, and then up to Abel-Keramim, slaughtering a huge number. So the Ammonites were subdued by the presence of the Israelites.
\s1 Jephthah’s daughter
\p
\v 34 When Jephthah got back to his house in the Mitspah area, look, his daughter was coming out to meet him with tambourines and with dancing. She was their only child—he didn’t have any son or daughter apart from her.
\v 35 When he saw her, he tore his clothes and said, “Oh no! My daughter! Indeed, you’ve made me bow in grief, and you’re among those who cause me pain, because I made a vow to Yahweh and I can’t go back on it.”
\p
\v 36 Then she said to him, “My father, you’ve made a vow to Yahweh. Do to me whatever you promised, after what Yahweh has done for you—total vengeance against your Ammonite enemies.”
\v 37 But she asked her father, “Just do this one thing for me: release me for two months, so I can go away and take some companions. I’ll fall prostrate on the hills and I’ll weep because I’ll never have a chance to marry.”
\v 38 He agreed and then sent her away for two months. She went with some companions, and she wept on the hills because she’d never be a mother.
\v 39 Then at the end of the two months, she returned to her father and he carried out his vow with respect to her so indeed she never had a chance to marry.
\p \add After that,\add* it became a custom in Israel
\v 40 that the young women of Israel go to commemorate the daughter of Jephthah the Giladi for four days during the year.
\c 12
\s1 Jephthah and the Efrayimim
\p
\v 1 Now the men of Ephraim were summoned and they passed northward and they said to Jephthah, “Why did you pass through in order to fight against the Ammonites, but you didn’t call us to go with you? We’re going to burn your house down over you!”
\p
\v 2 “I’ve been involved in very intense disputes—my people against the Ammonites,” Jephthah told them. “When I summoned you all, you didn’t \add come and\add* rescue me from them.
\v 3 When I could see that you weren’t coming to assist, I took my life into my own hands. I advanced against the Ammonites, and Yahweh gave me victory over them. So why then have you all come here to fight against me today?”
\v 4 Then Jephthah assembled all of the men of Gilead and they battled against Ephraim. The men of Gilead struck Ephraim because they had said, “You Gilead people are just fugitives from Ephraim—\add living here\add* in the middle of Ephraim, in the middle of Manasseh.”
\v 5 The Giladim captured the crossing places of the Jordan along Ephraim. Then when any of the survivors from Ephraim would say, “May I cross over,” the men of Gilead would ask, “Are you an Efrati?” If he said, “No,”
\v 6 then they’d demand, “Now say: Shibboleth.” But if he said “Sibboleth” because he didn’t have time to think to pronounce it correctly, they would seize him and would slaughter him at those crossing places of the Jordan. Forty-two thousand from Ephraim fell at that time.
\p
\v 7 Jephthah the Giladi led Israel for six years, then he died and was buried among the cities of Gilead.
\s1 Ivtsan, Eylon, and Avdon
\p
\v 8 Then after him, Ivtsan of Bethlehem led Israel.
\v 9 Now he had thirty sons. He gave away thirty daughters to be married to outsiders, and he brought in thirty daughters for his sons from the outside. Ivtsan led Israel for seven years,
\v 10 and when he died, he was buried in Bethlehem.
\p
\v 11 After him, Eylon the Zevuluni led Israel for ten years.
\v 12 When he died, he was buried in Ayalon in Zebulun.
\v 13 After him, Avdon, the son of Hilel the Pireatoni led Israel.
\v 14 He had forty sons and thirty grandsons, who rode on seventy young male donkeys. He led Israel for eight years.
\v 15 Then Avdon (the son of Hilel the Pireatoni) died, and he was buried in Pireaton in Ephraim, in the hill country of the Amalekites.
\c 13
\s1 Samson’s birth
\p
\v 1 The Israelites continued to do what Yahweh considered as evil, so he allowed the Philistines to oppress them for forty years.
\v 2 Now there was a man named Manoah from Tsor’ah, of the families of the Danim. His wife was barren so they had no children.
\v 3 \add One day,\add* Yahweh’s messenger appeared to the wife and told her, “Listen, please, you are barren and have never given birth, but you will conceive and have a son.
\v 4 Now take care that you surely don’t drink wine or strong drink, and that you don’t eat anything prohibited by our rules,
\v 5 because you’ll definitely become pregnant and will give birth to a son. His hair must never be cut because he’ll be a Nazirite to God from his conception. He will begin to rescue Israel from the oppression of the Philistines.”
\p
\v 6 Then the woman went and explained to her husband, “A man of God came to me. He looked like one of God’s messengers—extremely terrifying—so I didn’t \add like to\add* ask him where he’d come from, and he didn’t tell me his name.
\v 7 Then he told me, ‘You’re definitely pregnant and you’ll give birth to a son. So now, you mustn’t drink wine or strong drink, and you mustn’t eat anything prohibited by our rules, because the boy will be a Nazirite to God from conception until the day of his death.’ ”
\p
\v 8 Then Manoah prayed to Yahweh, “Oh please, my master, please let the man of God that you sent \add before\add*, come \add back\add* to us again so he can instruct us about what we must do for this boy who’s going to be born.”
\v 9 The true God listened to Manoah’s request, and God’s messenger came to the woman again when she was sitting \add out\add* in the field, but her husband Manoah wasn’t with her,
\v 10 so she quickly ran and told her husband, “Look. The man has appeared \add again\add* who came to me that day.”
\v 11 So Manoah followed his wife, and \add when\add* he came to the man, he asked him, “Are you the man who spoke to my wife \add before\add*?”
\p “\add Yes,\add* it was me,” he replied.
\p
\v 12 “Now may your words come true,” said Manoah. “How should we bring up the boy, and what will his work be?”
\p
\v 13 “Your wife must carefully follow everything I told her,” Yahweh’s messenger replied.
\v 14 “She mustn’t eat or drink anything that comes from the grapevine. She mustn’t drink wine or strong drink, and she mustn’t eat anything prohibited by our rules. She must do everything that I’ve instructed her.”
\p
\v 15 “Please stay a little longer,” Manoah asked Yahweh’s messenger, “so that we can prepare a meal of tender goat meat to honour your presence.”
\p
\v 16 “Even if I stayed longer, I wouldn’t eat your food,” Yahweh’s messenger replied. “But if you wanted to prepare a burnt offering for Yahweh, you may offer it.” (Because Manoah didn’t know that the messenger had come from Yahweh.)
\v 17 “What’s your name,” Manoah asked, “so that we can honour you when your words come true?”
\p
\v 18 “Why are you asking about my name,” Yahweh’s messenger replied. “You simply couldn’t understand.”
\p
\v 19 Then Manoah got their young goat and \add the other requirements for\add* the offering, and he presented a sacrifice to Yahweh on the rock. Then an amazing thing happened\f + \fr 13:19 \ft We’ve changed this to a passive construction here, because it’s not specified whether it was Yahweh himself or his messenger who did the miracle.\f* while Manoah and his wife watched—
\v 20 when the flames went up into the sky from the altar, Yahweh’s messenger went up in the flames while Manoah and his wife were watching. When they saw that, they quickly bowed right down to the ground.
\v 21 \add After that,\add* Manoah and his wife never saw him again, but now Manoah knew that he was Yahweh’s messenger.
\p
\v 22 Manoah said to his wife, “We’ll definitely die now because we’ve seen God!”
\p
\v 23 But his wife replied to him, “If Yahweh had wanted to kill us, he wouldn’t have accepted the entire burnt offering and the grain offering. He wouldn’t have shown us all those things, and he wouldn’t have given us those instructions.”
\p
\v 24 \add In due course,\add* the woman gave birth to a son and she named him Samson. The boy grew up and Yahweh blessed him,
\v 25 and then Yahweh’s spirit began to stir him \add when he was\add* in Mahaneh Dan—between Tsor’ah and between Eshtaol.
\c 14
\s1 Samson and the woman from Timnah
\p
\v 1 \add One time when\add* Samson went to Timnah, he noticed a young, Philistine woman there,
\v 2 and he went back home and he reported to his parents, “I saw a Philistine woman in Timnah that I’d like you to get for me as my wife.”
\p
\v 3 “Isn’t there a woman among your relatives?” his parents asked him, “Or among all of our people? Why take a wife from the uncircumcised Philistines?”
\p “Get her for me,” Samson said to his father, “because I feel she’s right for me.”
\p
\v 4 Now his parents didn’t know that Yahweh was arranging this, because he was seeking an opportunity against the Philistines who were oppressing Israel at that time.
\p
\v 5 Then Samson went to Timnah with his parents and \add when\add* they got to the vineyards there, wow, a young lion roared when it saw him.
\v 6 Yahweh’s spirit rose up in Samson, and he tore the lion to pieces with his bare hands like butchering a young goat, but he didn’t mention to his parents what he’d done.
\p
\v 7 Then he continued on and talked with the woman, and he really liked her.
\v 8 Some time later when he returned for the wedding, he turned off the path to look for the lion’s carcass, and to his surprise, a swarm of bees had nested in the carcass and \add there was\add* honey \add there\add*.
\v 9 He filled his cupped hands with honey, and continued along—walking and eating as he went to where his parents were. He even gave some to them to try, but he didn’t tell them that he had scooped it out of the carcass of a dead lion.
\v 10 Then his father went to visit the woman, and in the custom of the young men \add of the time\add*, Samson held a party \add there\add*.
\v 11 When they saw him, they brought thirty close friends to join him.\f + \fr 14:11 \ft Although we can understand the words here, we’re not certain of the implications, or perhaps the cultural traditions. Did Samson appear too alone? Too intimidating? Or was it just bringing more friends for good food and drink?\f*
\v 12 Samson said to them, “Let me suggest a riddle for you all. If you’re \add able to\add* explain it to me during the seven days of the party and you’ve worked it out correctly, I’ll give you all thirty linen coats and thirty sets of clothes.
\v 13 But if you all can’t explain it to me, then you yourselves must give me thirty linen coats and thirty sets of clothes.”
\p “Okay, we’re listening. Tell us the riddle.” they answered.
\p
\v 14 So he said,
\q1 “Food came out of the eater,
\q1 and sweetness came out of the strong one.”
\m But three days later, they still couldn’t solve his riddle.
\p
\v 15 Then on the seventh day,\f + \fr 14:15 \ft The Greek Septuagint translation says ‘the \+em fourth\+em* day’, which perhaps might fit better into the narrative, but ‘seventh’ is in the Hebrew that we have and not necessarily incorrect.\f* they pressured Samson’s wife, “Get your husband to tell you the answer of the riddle, otherwise we’ll burn \add down\add* your father’ house and you \add as well\add*. Did you invite us to the party just to impoverish us?”
\p
\v 16 Then Samson’s wife put on a crying act and said, “You must hate me—you don’t love me \add because\add* you’ve created a riddle for my relatives, yet you haven’t even told me the answer.”
\p “Listen,” he replied, “I haven’t \add even\add* told my own parents. Should I tell you?”
\v 17 She continued to cry beside him throughout the seven-day party, and then \add finally\add* on the seventh day, he \add gave in and\add* told her because she kept nagging him, \add so in the end\add* she was able to reveal the answer to her relatives.
\v 18 Then the men of the city said to Samson before the sun went down on the seventh day,
\q1 “What is sweeter than honey?
\q1 And what’s stronger than a lion?”
\m So he said to them,
\q1 “If you all hadn’t ploughed with my heifer,
\q1 you wouldn’t have been able to solve my riddle.”
\p
\v 19 Then Yahweh’s spirit rose up in him, and he went to Ashkelon and killed thirty men \add there\add*. He took their possessions, and he gave the changes of clothes for the riddle. But he was very angry, so he went back to his parent’s home.
\v 20 Then \add (unknown to Samson)\add*, his wife was given to the man who’d been his close friend and companion.
\c 15
\p
\v 1 Some days later during the wheat harvest, Samson visited his wife with a young goat \add as a present for the family, because\add* he said, “I’ll go into the bedroom with my wife.” But her father wouldn’t allow him to go in,
\v 2 and pleaded, “Truly, I thought that you must hate her, so I gave her to your close friend. But wouldn’t her younger sister be better \add anyway\add*? Please, would you take her instead.”
\p
\v 3 Samson responded, “This time I’m blameless concerning the Philistines when I cause trouble for them.”
\v 4 Then he went and captured three hundred foxes and tied them in pairs—tail to tail. He took torches, and tied one torch between the tails of each pair.
\v 5 He lit the torches and let the foxes loose into the grain crops of the Philistines. As a result, he burnt both the standing grain \add plants\add* and also the \add harvested\add* stacks of sheaves, and even a vineyard and an olive orchard.
\v 6 So the Philistines asked, “Who did this?”
\p “Samson,” they replied, “the son-in-law of the man from Timnah, because he took Samson’s wife and gave her to his close friend.” As a result, the Philistines went \add to Timnah\add* and they burnt \add both\add* the woman and her father.
\v 7 Then Samson told them, “Since you \add actually\add* did that \add horrible thing\add*, I certainly won’t stop until I’ve avenged myself against you all.”
\v 8 Then he attacked the Philistines and crippled many of them, before going to Eytam’s rock where he lived in a cave.
\s1 Samson defeats the Philistines
\p
\v 9 However, the Philistine \add warriors\add* went and camped in Judah, and spread out \add around the town of\add* Lehi.
\v 10 But the men of Judah asked them, “Why have you all come here to attack us?”
\p “We’ve come to capture Samson,” they replied. “To give him some of his own back.”
\v 11 Then three thousand men from Judah went down to the crevice in Eytam’s rock, and told Samson, “Don’t you realise that the Philistines are ruling over us? Why are you causing more trouble for us?”
\p “Just as they’ve done to me,” he replied, “so I’ve done to them.”
\p
\v 12 “We’ve come here to tie you up so we can hand you over to the Philistines,” they said.
\p “\add Okay,\add*” Samson responded. “\add But\add* promise me that you all won’t injure me yourselves.”
\p
\v 13 “No, however, we’ll certainly tie you up,” they replied, “and we’ll hand you over to them. But truly we won’t kill you.” Then they tied him up with two new ropes and brought him up from the rocks.
\p
\v 14 When they’d taken him as far as Lehi, the Philistines shouted \add triumphantly\add* when they saw him. Then Yahweh’s spirit rose in Samson and the ropes around his arms just seemed as weak as burnt flax stalks when he simply snapped them off him.
\v 15 He saw a fresh donkey’s jawbone \add lying on the ground\add*, and he reached out his hand and grabbed it, and he slaughtered a thousand \add Philistine\add* men with it.
\v 16 Then Samson declared,
\q1 “With a donkey’s jawbone, one heap, two heaps,
\q1 with a donkey’s jawbone, I’ve slaughtered a thousand men.”
\m
\v 17 When he finished reciting that, he hurled the jawbone away, and he called that place ‘Ramat-Lehi’ \add (which means ‘jawbone hill’)\add*.
\p
\v 18 Then he was very thirsty and cried out to Yahweh, “You yourself have given your servant this great victory, but now must I die with this thirst and get captured by those uncircumcised \add Philistines\add*?”
\v 19 So God split open the \add little\add* hollow that’s at Lehi, and water came out of it. When he drank, his strength returned and he revived. Because of that, he called it ‘Eyn-Hakore’ \add (which means ‘the fountain of the one who called out’)\add*, which is in Lehi to this day.
\v 20 And \add so\add* Samson led Israel for twenty years at the time of the Philistine \add oppression\add*.
\c 16
\s1 Samson carries away the gates
\p
\v 1 Samson went to Gaza \add city\add* and he saw a prostitute woman there so he went in to spend the night with her.
\v 2 But the Gazim passed around the news, “Samson’s in town.” So they encircled the city gate and they laid in wait for him all night. They stayed quiet the whole night, saying, “We’ll kill him when it gets light.”
\v 3 Samson stayed with her until the middle of the night, then he got up. He picked up both the gates including the cross-bar and their posts, and hoisted them up onto his shoulders, and he carried them up to the top of the hill which is in front of Hebron.
\s1 Samson and Delilah
\p
\v 4 Sometime after that, Samson fell in love with a \add Philistine\add* woman named Delilah who \add lived in\add* the Sorek valley.
\v 5 The Philistine leaders came to her and requested, “Make him open up. Find out what makes him strong, and how we can beat him, so that we can tie him up to keep him in our power. Then we’ll each give you one thousand one hundred silver coins.”
\p
\v 6 So Delilah asked Samson, “Please, tell me what makes you so strong, and how could someone tie you up to keep you in their power?”
\p
\v 7 “If they’d tie me up with seven green cords that haven’t been dried,” he replied, “then I’d become weak and be just like any other man.”
\p
\v 8 So the Philistine leaders brought her seven green cords that hadn’t been dried, and she tied him up with them.
\v 9 Now she had an ambush \add group\add* ready in the \add next\add* room when she said to him, “The Philistines are here to get you, Samson!” But he tore the cords to shreds just like a strip of flax fiber is torn to shreds when the fire reaches it, thus they didn’t find out \add the secret of\add* his strength.
\v 10 Then Delilah said to him, “Listen, you deceived me and lied to me. Now tell me, please, how could someone tie you up?”
\p
\v 11 “Actually, if they would tie me with new ropes which have never been used,” he replied, “I’d become weak and be just like any other man.”
\p
\v 12 So Delilah took new cords and tied him up with them. She said to him, “The Philistines are here to get you, Samson!” with the ambush group staying in the \add next\add* room. But he flicked them off his arms like they were \add just\add* thread.
\p
\v 13 Then Delilah said to Samson, “So far you’ve \add just\add* been deceitful to me and told me lies. \add Now\add* Tell me how you can be tied up.”
\p So he told her, “\add It’s\add* if you just weave seven locks of my hair into the loom.”
\p
\v 14 So \add she did it and\add* fastened it with the pin, and called out, “The Philistines are here to get you, Samson!” Then he woke up and ripped out the pin, the loom, and \add his hair from\add* the fabric.
\p
\v 15 Then she said to him, “How can you say, ‘I love you,’ when your heart isn’t with me? You’ve been deceitful to me three times \add now\add*, and you haven’t revealed to me the secret of your amazing strength.”
\v 16 Day after day she nagged and pestered him until he was sick to death of hearing it.
\v 17 So \add finally\add* he told her his secret, “My hair has never been cut because I’ve been a Nazirite to God since my birth. If my head would be shaved, then my strength would leave me, and I’d become weak—I’d be just like any other man.”
\p
\v 18 When Delilah perceived that he’d told her his deepest secret, she summoned the Philistine leaders, “Come here one more time, because he’s told me his secret.” So the Philistine leaders came, bringing the silver \add coins\add* with them.
\v 19 Then she made him fall asleep on her knees, and she called in a man to shave off his seven locks of hair, causing him to start to weaken, and his \add additional\add* strength left him.
\v 20 Then she said, “The Philistines are here to get you, Samson!” He woke up and thought to himself, “I’ll escape like all the other times and I’ll shake myself loose.” But he didn’t know that Yahweh had left him.
\v 21 So the Philistines seized him, and they gouged out his eyes. They brought him to Gaza and chained him with a pair of bronze shackles, then they put him to work in prison grinding \add grain\add*.
\v 22 However, \add over time\add* his hair began to grow back again.
\s1 Samson’s death
\p
\v 23 \add Some time later,\add* the Philistine leaders gathered together for a celebration and to offer a large sacrifice to their god Dagon, saying, “Our god has helped us capture our terrible enemy, Samson.”
\v 24 When the people saw him, they praised their god, saying, “Our god has helped us capture our enemy—the devastator of our region who killed so many of us.”
\v 25 Later in the celebration when the drink had liberated their thinking, they said, “Call for Samson so he can entertain us.” So they brought Samson in from the prison and \add taunted him\add* to get them all laughing. They made him stand among the pillars \add of the building\add*
\v 26 and he asked the servant who was guiding him by the hand, “Place my hands against the two pillars holding up the building so I can lean on them.”
\v 27 The building was full of the men and the women, including the Philistine leaders. On the roof there were about three thousand men and women watching the entertainment with Samson.
\p
\v 28 Then Samson called out to Yahweh, “My master Yahweh, please remember me. Strengthen me please, just this time, oh God, so that I can avenge myself by one \add last\add* act of vengeance on the Philistines \add to make them pay for\add* my two eyes.”
\v 29 Then he took hold of the two centre pillars on which the building depended, and pressed out against them—one in his right hand and one in his left.
\v 30 Then he exclaimed, “Let me die with the Philistines!” He stretched out with his strength and the building fell on their leaders and all of the people in it. So he killed more people at his death than he had killed during his life.
\p
\v 31 Then his brothers and all his father’s household went \add to Gaza\add* and carried his body back. They buried him between Tsor’ah and Eshtaol in the burial place of his father Manoah. He had helped Israel for twenty years.
\c 17
\s1 The false gods of Mikiyas
\p
\v 1 Now there was a man named Micah from the Ephraim hill country
\v 2 who admitted to his mother, “Those eleven hundred silver coins that were taken from you—when you made a curse, and even said it when I was listening, \add well\add* listen, I’ve got it all—I took it myself.”
\p His mother said, “May Yahweh bless you, my son.”
\v 3 Then he brought back the eleven hundred silver coins to his mother and she declared, “I will certainly consecrate the money to Yahweh for my son’s benefit. Take it and we’ll use it to have one statue carved and another cast.”
\v 4 So after the money had been returned, his mother gave two hundred silver coins to a silversmith. He made a carved figure and one cast \add from a mould\add*, and \add they were placed\add* in Micah’s house.
\p
\v 5 Now that Micah had a shrine with gods in it, he made some priestly robes and some household gods, and consecrated one of his sons as his priest.
\v 6 In those days, Israel didn’t have a king—each person would do whatever seemed right to them.
\p
\v 7 Now there was a young Levite man from the family of Judah\f + \fr 17:7 \ft Maybe one parent was from the Levi tribe and one from Judah?\f* who’d been staying in Bethlehem in Judah).
\v 8 He left Bethlehem to find another place to say, and on his way, he ended up arriving at Micah’s place in the Efrati hill country.
\v 9 “Where have you come from?” Micah asked him.
\p “I’m a Levite from Bethlehem in Judah,” he replied, “and I’m trying to find a good place to stay a while.”
\p
\v 10 “Stay with me,” said Micah, “and be like a father and a priest to me. I’ll give you ten silver coins a year as well as board and clothing.” So the Levite \add man\add* went in
\v 11 and agreed to stay with Micah, \add eventually\add* becoming like one of Micah’s \add own\add* sons.
\v 12 Micah ordained the Levite, and the young man became like a priest to him, and lived in his house.
\v 13 “Now I know that Yahweh will make everything go well for me,” Micah said, “because I have this Levite as a priest.”
\c 18
\s1 The Danim settle in Layish
\p
\v 1 In those days, Israel didn’t have a king. The Dan tribe was looking for land to settle in, because up until then, they hadn’t been able to claim their allocated region among the Israelite tribes.
\v 2 So the Danim sent out five capable men (from Tsor’ah and from Eshtaol) to scout out the region and explore it. They had told them, “Go \add and\add* explore the area.” They came to the Efrati hill country, as far as Micah’s house, and spent the night there.
\v 3 They’d been near Micah’s house when they recognized the voice of the young Levite man. So they’d gone in there and they asked him, “Who brought you here? What are you doing in this \add place\add*? What do you get here?”
\p
\v 4 Then he told them what Micah had done for him, and how he’d hired him to be like his priest.
\p
\v 5 So they asked him, “Please find out from God if we’ll have success on this expedition we’re on.”
\p
\v 6 “Continue on in peace,” the priest said. “Yahweh has prepared everything ahead of you all.”
\p
\v 7 Then the five men left and went on to Layish. They saw that the people there were living in confidence just like the Sidonians were—peacefully and \add feeling\add* secure. No one in the region was causing trouble for them and they were very settled. They were far away from the Sidonians and didn’t interact with others.
\v 8 Then they went \add back\add* to their relatives in Tsor’ah and Eshtaol, who asked them, “How did it go?”
\v 9 “Get ready immediately,” they said, “so that we can go \add to battle\add* against them! Because we’ve seen the land and listen, it’s incredibly good. Don’t just stay still here—you all need to take action to go in to take possession of the land.
\p
\v 10 When you go, you’ll all come to a people living without suspicion of attack, and the land is spacious in all directions, because God will hand it over to you—a place where there’s no lack of anything that’s on the earth.”
\p
\v 11 So six hundred Dani warriors left Tsor’ah and Eshtaol, carrying their weapons.
\v 12 They set off and camped at Kiryat-Yearim in Judah. That’s why they call that place Mahaneh-Dan \add (meaning ‘Dan’s camp’)\add* to this day—it’s back behind Kiryat-Yearim.
\v 13 From there, they crossed over into the Efrati hill country, \add eventually\add* arriving near Micah’s house.
\p
\v 14 Then the five men who had been here previously scouting out the land said to their group, “Do you know that there’s a house here with a gear for priests, family idols, a carved image, and a cast image? So now, consider what you should do.”
\v 15 So they turned off the main path and they came to the place with the young Levite man in Micah’s house, and they asked about his welfare.
\v 16 The six hundred Dani men stayed outside at the gate, holding their weapons.
\p
\v 17 The five men who’d gone to scout out the land went in \add around the back\add*. They took the carved image, the priest’s gear, the family idols, and the cast image, while the priest was standing at the entrance of the gate along with the six hundred armed men.
\v 18 When the priest saw them bringing out all the religious objects, he demanded, “What’s going on?”
\p
\v 19 “Stay quiet,” they told him. “Put your hand over your mouth and come with us, and be like a father and a priest for us. Is it better to be a priest for \add only\add* one household, or to be a priest for a \add whole\add* related tribe in Israel?”
\v 20 The priest liked that idea, so he went along with them—taking the priestly gear and the household idols, and the carved image.
\p
\v 21 Then they \add went back to the main path\add* and continued on their way with the children, animals, and \add those carrying\add* the valuable property going in front.
\v 22 When they’d already gone quite some distance from Micah’s house, Micah and his neighbours gathered together, then the men ran \add in a group\add* and caught up with the Danim.
\v 23 They called out to the Danim who turned around to face them and asked Micah, “What’s the matter? Why has this group come together?”
\v 24 “You all took my gods that I made,” Micah replied, “And the priest. Then you disappeared and I had nothing left. So how can you all ask me what the matter is!”
\p
\v 25 “I wouldn’t say too much if I was you,” he was told. “Otherwise some of our more fiery men might start to get a bit hostile, then you’d bring about your own death, and that of your companions!”
\v 26 Then Micah realised that they were too strong for them, so they turned back around and went home.
\p So the Danim continued onward,
\v 27 taking with them the things that Micah had made, as well as his priest. They came to Layish where the unsuspecting people were living peaceably, and they slaughtered them with their swords and burnt down the city.
\v 28 No one came to rescue them because it was a long way from Sidon and they did have much involvement with them anyway. (Layish was in the valley near Beyt-Rehov.) They rebuilt the city and then moved into it.
\v 29 They named the \add rebuilt\add* city Dan (instead of Layish) in honour of their ancestor Dan who was one of Israel’s sons.
\v 30 The Danim mounted the carved image for themselves \add to worship\add*. Jonathan Gereshom’s son from Manasseh became the priest for the Danim (and then his descendants until when the people were \add eventually\add* exiled from the region).
\v 31 So they set up the carved image that Micah had made \add and worshipped it\add* the whole time that God’s tent was at Shiloh.
\c 19
\s1 The Levite and his slave-wife
\p
\v 1 In those days when there was no king over Israel, there was a Levite man who was staying in the remotest parts of the Ephraim hill country. He took a slave-wife for himself from Bethlehem in Judah.
\v 2 However, she started sleeping around, and then she left him and went to her father’s house in Bethlehem \add where she ended up\add* staying for four months.
\v 3 Then her husband got organised and went after her—to sweet-talk her and persuade her to return with him. He took a young \add servant\add* man and a team of donkeys. She invited her husband into her father’s house, and when her father saw him, he was glad to meet him.
\v 4 His father-in-law (her father) insisted on offering hospitality, so they ate and drank, and he stayed the night, and \add ended up\add* staying for three days.
\v 5 Then on the fourth day, they got up early and the Levite prepared to leave, but his father-in-law urged him to eat before they left.
\p
\v 6 So the two of them ate and drank together, and then the woman’s father said, “Please stay \add another\add* night. Relax and have a good time.”
\p
\v 7 The man got ready to leave, however his father-in-law persisted, so he stayed \add another\add* night there.
\v 8 He got up early in the morning on the fifth day to get ready to leave, but the father of the young woman said, “Get some energy first.” So they waited until later in the day, and then the two of them ate.
\p
\v 9 When the man stood up to go with his slave-wife and his young man, his father-in-law said, “Listen, the day’s coming to an end soon. Please stay the night. See, it’ll be getting dark soon. Spend the night here and be happy. Then make an early start tomorrow for your journey home.”
\p
\v 10 But the man \add from the Levi tribe\add* didn’t want to stay another night. He put saddles on his two donkeys, and started to go with his slave-wife \add and his servant\add* toward Yevus \add city\add* (now called Jerusalem.
\p
\v 11 They got close to Yevus and the daylight had mostly gone, so the young man said to his master, “Come, please, we should turn aside into this city of the Jebusites so we can spend the night there.”
\v 12 “We won’t go into a city of foreigners,” his master replied. “There’s no Israelites there. Better to keep going until we reach Giveah.”
\v 13 Then he added, “Yes, let’s keep going to reach either Giveah or Ramah to spend the night in.”
\v 14 So they passed \add Yevus\add* and went on, but the sun went down as they neared Giveah (in Benyamite \add territory\add*)
\v 15 so they went into Giveah to spend the night there. They went and sat in the open plaza of the city, but no one invited them into their home for the night.
\p
\v 16 But then in \add late\add* evening, an old man came in from his work in the field. He was from the Efrati hill country but \add currently\add* staying in the Giveah area where the residents were Benyamites.
\v 17 He looked up and noticed them in the open plaza of the city and asked the man, “Where are you going, and where have you come from?”
\p
\v 18 “We’re passing through from Bethlehem in Judah,” the Levite replied. “Going to the remote parts of the Efrati hill country where I’m from. I went to Bethlehem, and I’m going to \add Shiloh where\add* Yahweh’s house is. However, no one here has invited us into their home.
\v 19 \add We have our own\add* food and straw for our donkeys, and \add enough\add* food and wine for the three of us \add so\add* we don’t need anything else.
\p
\v 20 “Peace to you all,” said the old man. “Let me supply everything you need—only don’t spend the night \add here\add* in the square.”
\v 21 Then he took them to his house and fed their donkeys. They washed their feet, and they \add all\add* ate and drank.
\v 22 They were having a good time, but suddenly the men of the city—wicked men—had surrounded the house—pounding repeatedly on the door. They called out to the old man who owned the house, “\add Hey,\add* bring out the man who came to your place, so we can have fun with him.”\f + \fr 19:22 \ft Of course, everyone understood that their aim was to sodomise him.\f*
\v 23 The man who owned the house went out to them and said to them, “No, my brothers, please you mustn’t act wickedly. That man is a guest in my house—you all mustn’t do this disgraceful thing.
\v 24 My virgin daughter and his slave-wife are here. Please let me bring \em them\em* out so you can ravish them and do whatever you want to them. But don’t do that wicked thing to that man.”
\v 25 But the men weren’t willing to listen to him, so the man pushed his slave-wife out to them and they raped her and abused her the whole night until the morning, when they discarded her as it began to dawn.
\p
\v 26 The woman managed to get herself back to the house where her master was, and collapsed at the entrance until it got light.
\v 27 When her master got up and he opened the door to go and continue on his trip, look, his slave-wife had fallen at the entrance and her hands were on the threshold.
\v 28 “Get up,” he told her. “Let’s go.” But she didn’t answer, so he put her on his donkey and headed home.
\v 29 When he arrived, he got a knife and hacked his wife’s \add body\add* into twelve pieces (including the bones), and he sent them to various places spread across all of Israel’s territory.
\v 30 Then everyone who saw a piece said, “Nothing has \add ever\add* happened like this before—we’ve never seen anything like this from the time that the Israelites left Egypt until now. What do we need to do about it? Speak up and make a plan.”
\c 20
\s1 The Israelites prepare for war
\p
\v 1 Then all the Israelites from Dan \add in the north\add* to Beersheba \add in the south\add*, and \add even\add* from Gilead \add across the Jordan\add*, came and assembled before Yahweh at Mitspah with one single, united purpose.
\v 2 The leaders of all the Israelite tribes also came out to show their agreement with the assembly of the people of the true God: four hundred thousand warriors all carrying swords.
\p
\v 3 Meanwhile, the Benyamites had heard that the Israelites had gathered at Mitspah, and everyone asked, “Explain how this wicked thing came to happen.”
\p
\v 4 So the Levite man (the husband of the woman who had been murdered) answered, “I went to the Giveah \add region\add* (in Benjamin) with my slave-wife to spend the night.
\v 5 The citizens of Giveah conspired \add together\add* against me. They surrounded the house at night because of me, intending to kill me. Instead, they assaulted \add and abused\add* my slave-wife \add so badly that\add* she died.
\v 6 I took my slave-wife’s \add body\add* back \add home\add* and cut it into pieces, and I sent them out into every section of the territory that God gave Israel, because they’ve committed a heinous crime, and a disgrace to Israel.
\v 7 Listen, all of you Israelites, what’s your advice? What should we do about it?.”
\p
\v 8 All the people agreed together and started saying, “We won’t go back to our families. We won’t return to our houses.
\v 9 rather this is what we must do to Giveah: choose by lot who will fight against them.
\v 10 Choose a tenth of our men to \add go and\add* get supplies \add for the other warriors to travel\add* to Giveah in Benjamin \add after what they did that\add* outraged Israel.
\v 11 So all Israel was united together against that city.
\p
\v 12 Then the Israelite tribes sent men across the Benyamite region to ask, “What’s this evil thing that’s been done among you?
\v 13 So now, hand over those worthless men from the Giveah \add region\add* so that we can put them to death and purge Israel of that evil.” But the Benyamites weren’t willing to take the advice of their Israelite relatives.
\v 14 They came out of their cities \add and assembled\add* at Giveah to the battle against the Israelites.
\v 15 The Benyamites mobilised together twenty-six thousand men from their cities on that day, all carrying swords. In addition, seven hundred chosen men had been mobilised from the Giveah \add region\add*.
\v 16 Among all of those people were seven hundred chosen left-handers, each of whom could sling a stone at a hair without missing.
\v 17 The Israelites (excluding Benyamites) mustered up for themselves four hundred thousand men—all expert warriors and all carrying swords.
\s1 The war to punish the Benyamites
\p
\v 18 The Israelites got ready and went to Bethel and asked God, “Who should lead us into battle with the Benyamites?”
\p “It should be Judah out front,” Yahweh responded.
\p
\v 19 So the Israelites set out in the morning and made camp near Giveah.
\v 20 Then they went out to battle against the Benyamites—setting themselves up for the battle against them at Giveah.
\p
\v 21 But the Benyamites led a surprise attack out of Giveah and killed twenty-two thousand of the Israelite warriors.
\v 22 However the Israelites strengthened themselves and they continued to set up for battle the next day at the same place.
\p
\v 23 Then the Israelites assembled and wept before Yahweh until evening. They asked him, “Should we continue to attack and battle against our cousins the Benyamites?”
\p “Go back against them,” Yahweh replied.
\v 24 So the Israelites went back to battle against the Benyamites \add again\add* on the second day.
\v 25 \add Again\add* the Benyamites came out from Giveah, and \add this time\add* they killed eighteen thousand Israelite swordsmen.
\v 26 Then the Israelite warriors and all the people went back to Bethel. They wept, and they sat there before Yahweh. They fasted that day until the evening, then they offered burnt offerings and peace offerings to Yahweh.
\v 27 They asked Yahweh (because the box containing the agreement with the true God was there in those days
\v 28 and Pinhas (son of Aaron’s son Eleazar) was serving before it in those days), “Should we continue to go to the battle once more against our cousins the Benyamites, or should we stop?”
\p “Go back \add again\add*, because tomorrow I’ll give you victory over them,” Yahweh responded.
\p
\v 29 So the Israelites placed groups of warriors in ambush \add all\add* around Giveah.
\v 30 Then they took their positions \add now\add* for the third day against the Benyamites in Giveah.
\v 31 When the Benyamites came out from the city, they were lured away as they began to strike down their enemies like the time before. Around thirty Israelites were killed on the \add two\add* roads—one going to Bethel and the other to Giveah through the countryside.
\v 32 Then the Benyamites said, “We’re starting to defeat them, just like before.”
\p But the Israelites had said, “Let’s flee so that we can draw them away from the city \add and\add* onto the roads.”
\v 33 Then \add the main group\add* of Israelites retreated and set up battle lines at Baal-Tamar, as the ones hiding in ambush made a surprise attack from their places in Ma’areh-Geva.
\v 34 Ten thousand chosen Israelite warriors came out in the sight of the Giveah \add region\add*, and the battle was fierce, but the Benyamites hadn’t yet realised that disaster was about to hit them.
\v 35 Yahweh \add helped the Israelites to defeat the Benyamites\add* that day, and over twenty-five thousand \add Benyamite\add* swordsmen were killed
\v 36 and they saw that they were defeated.
\p The Israelites had given ground to the Benyamites because they had relied on the ambush placed around Giveah.
\v 37 Then the group hiding in ambush had rushed out and spread out around Giveah, then had gone in and attacked the entire city with their swords.
\v 38 Then they \add started a fire\add* as the prearranged signal was to be the rising column of smoke up out of the city.
\p
\v 39 Meanwhile the \add main\add* Israelite force had retreated, and the Benyamites had attacked and killed around thirty men, thinking that they were winning like they’d done \add twice\add* before.
\v 40 But when the pillar of smoke began to rise from the city, the Benyamites looked behind them and were shocked to see their city going up in smoke.
\v 41 Then the \add main\add* Israelite force \add stopped retreating and\add* turned back \add to attack again\add*. The Benyamites were horrified because they \add now\add* saw that disaster had struck them.
\v 42 They retreated away from the Israelites into the wilderness, but the battle overtook them, plus the Israelites were coming \add back\add* out of the cities getting right in amongst them \add to fight\add*.
\v 43 They surrounded the Benyamites as they tried to flee, and easily ran them down as they tried to escape from the Giveah region towards the east.
\v 44 Eighteen thousand strong Benyamite warriors were killed,
\v 45 and \add the rest\add* turned and fled into the wilderness towards the Rimon rock. Another five thousand men were killed along the trails. They chased them all the way to Gideom, and killed \add another\add* two thousand men,
\v 46 so the total number of Benyamite swordsmen who were killed came to twenty-five thousand—all strong warriors.
\p
\v 47 But six hundred men made it to Rimon rock, and they stayed there for four months.
\v 48 Meanwhile, the Israelites went back to the Benyamite \add region\add* and killed the people with their swords—entire cities including cattle and everything else—then the cities were set on fire.
\c 21
\s1 Wives for the Benyamites
\p
\v 1 Now \add back\add* in Mitspah, the Israelite men had promised never to allow their daughters to marry a Benyamite man.
\v 2 But \add now\add* the Israelites went to Bethel and sat there before God until evening—calling out and crying loudly,
\v 3 asking, “Oh Yahweh, God of Israel, why has this happened to Israel—for one tribe to be missing from Israel now?”
\p
\v 4 The next day, the people got up early and built an altar there, and they offered up burnt offerings and peace offerings.
\v 5 Then the Israelites asked, “Among all the Israelite tribes serving Yahweh, was there any group who didn’t join the battle?” (\add They asked that\add* because they had previously decided that anyone who didn’t go to Mitspah to fight for Yahweh would be put to death.)
\v 6 Now the Israelites felt sorry for their Benyamite cousins, saying, “An \add entire\add* tribe has been hacked off Israel today.
\v 7 How can we find wives for any \add Benyamite\add* men that are left because we’ve promised to Yahweh not to give our daughters to them to marry?
\p
\v 8 So they asked, “Is there any from our tribes who didn’t show up for Yahweh at Mitspah?” Then listen, no one from Yavesh-Gilead had joined their assembly \add there\add*.
\v 9 (When the people had checked around themselves, \add they’d realised that\add* no one from Yavesh-Gilead was there.)
\v 10 So the Israelites sent twelve thousand warriors there, commanding them to use their swords to kill the inhabitants of Yavesh-Gilead, including \add married\add* women and children—
\v 11 \add specifically\add* they had to kill every male, and every woman who’s slept with a male.
\v 12 \add That way\add* they discovered four hundred young virgin women among the inhabitants of Yavesh-Gilead, and brought them \add back\add* to their camp at Shiloh (in the Canaan \add region\add*).
\p
\v 13 Then the entire assembly sent a message to the Benyamites at Rimon rock, saying that they were now ready for peace.
\v 14 So the Benyamites returned and were given the young women from Yavesh-Gilead, but there wasn’t enough of them.
\p
\v 15 The people \add still\add* felt sorry for the Benyamites, because Yahweh had caused a gap among Israel’s tribes,
\v 16 so the leaders asked, “How can we provide wives for the remaining Benyamite men since \add the other\add* Benyamite women were wiped out?”
\v 17 \add Because\add* they said, “They must be able to procreate so that an entire Israelite tribe isn’t blotted out.
\v 18 But we can’t be the ones to give our daughters to them to marry because we made an oath that anyone who did that would be cursed.”
\p
\v 19 They said, “Listen, there’s a celebration for Yahweh from time to time at Shiloh, which is on the north of Bethel, east of the road that goes from Bethel uphill to Shechem, and on the south of Levonah.”
\v 20 So they told the Benyamites, “Go and hide in those vineyards
\v 21 and watch. When the Shiloh girls come out to perform in the dances, come out of the vineyards and you can each catch a wife for yourselves, then go \add back home\add* to Benjamin.
\v 22 When their fathers or brothers complain to us, we’ll tell them, “Let them have them since we didn’t find you for them. And you all won’t be guilty of giving them to the Benyamites \add because they stole them\add*.
\p
\v 23 So that’s what the Benyamites did—they grabbed enough dancing girls to be their wives, then they returned to their allocated land and rebuilt their cities and lived in them.
\v 24 The \add other\add* Israelites went back to their tribes and their families in the areas that had been allocated to them.
\p
\v 25 In those days, there was no king in Israel—everyone would \add just\add* do what seemed right to them.
